% Abstract
\chapter*{Abstract}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Abstract}
\thispagestyle{fancy}

In recent years, the Indian government has launched numerous welfare schemes targeting various sectors such as education, agriculture, healthcare, employment, and social justice. However, due to fragmented information across multiple government websites, accessing and understanding these schemes remains a challenge for the common citizen. 

The Government Scheme Portal is a centralized platform designed to address this gap. It aims to empower users—particularly those with limited digital literacy—by simplifying access to relevant scheme information through automated web scraping, intelligent search, and language support. The platform consolidates data from official government portals, processes it into a user-friendly format, and offers interactive chatbot-based assistance for queries.

By integrating speech-to-text, regional language support, and a responsive web interface, the portal enhances accessibility for rural populations, elderly citizens, and those with disabilities. The implementation of machine learning algorithms enables personalized scheme recommendations based on user profiles and eligibility criteria, significantly improving the likelihood of citizens finding relevant assistance programs. Additionally, the platform's intuitive visual design and step-by-step application guidance removes traditional barriers to welfare program enrollment.

Future development includes a mobile application to increase reach and usability across devices, offline functionality for areas with limited connectivity, and integration with digital identity verification to streamline application processes. This project represents a significant step toward improving digital governance and ensuring that the benefits of public welfare schemes are accessible to every eligible citizen regardless of their technical proficiency, language preference, or geographic location.

% Introduction
\chapter{Introduction}
\thispagestyle{fancy}

\section{Background and Context}
In India, numerous government schemes are launched to benefit different sections of society. However, this information is scattered across multiple government websites, making it difficult for citizens to find relevant schemes. Many citizens, especially those from rural areas and elderly populations, miss out on benefits they are eligible for simply due to information barriers.

\section{Motivation for the Project}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Many government websites are not user-friendly, especially for older citizens
    \item Lack of a centralized platform to search and filter schemes
    \item Existing platforms do not support regional languages, making it difficult for non-English speakers to access information
    \item Digital divide creates barriers for those with limited technical skills
    \item Underutilization of benefits due to information inaccessibility
    \item Growing smartphone penetration creates opportunity for mobile-friendly solutions
\end{itemize}

\section{Objectives and Scope}
The main goal is to create a user-friendly platform that allows users to:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Search for government schemes based on keywords
    \item Get personalized recommendations based on eligibility
    \item Use a chatbot to find relevant schemes through conversation
    \item Convert text into different local languages for better accessibility
    \item Enable speech-based queries for users who are not comfortable typing
    \item Provide clear eligibility criteria and application processes for each scheme
\end{itemize}

% Literature Review
\chapter{Literature Review}
\thispagestyle{fancy}

\section{Related Work}
Our examination of existing solutions reveals several approaches to providing government scheme information:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Government Portals:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item MyGov Portal: Focuses on citizen engagement but offers limited scheme discovery features
        \item National Portal of India: Provides information across departments but lacks user-centric organization
        \item PM Kisan Portal: Well-designed for a specific scheme but represents the fragmented approach
        \item State Government Websites: Inconsistent design and information architecture across states
    \end{itemize}
    
    \item Private Platforms:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item IndiaFilings: Provides scheme information with business services but limited to major schemes
        \item GovInfo by Factly: Consolidates information in journalistic format but lacks interactivity
    \end{itemize}
    
    \item Mobile Applications:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item UMANG: Combines multiple services but prioritizes delivery over discovery
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\section{Gaps in Existing Knowledge}
Based on our review, we identified several critical gaps:
\begin{itemize}
    \item No comprehensive aggregation of schemes across government levels
    \item Limited search intelligence and natural language understanding
    \item Absence of personalization based on user profiles
    \item Insufficient accessibility features for diverse user needs
    \item Lack of conversational interfaces for guided discovery
    \item Outdated information across many platforms
    \item Complex navigation structures that reflect government organization rather than user needs
\end{itemize}

% Problem Statement
\chapter{Problem Statement}
\thispagestyle{fancy}

The lack of a centralized, user-friendly, and accessible platform for discovering and understanding government schemes creates substantial barriers for Indian citizens attempting to access welfare benefits they are entitled to. This problem is particularly acute for digitally less confident users, speakers of regional languages, elderly citizens, and rural populations. The current ecosystem of fragmented portals with complex interfaces, limited search capabilities, and inconsistent language support results in eligible citizens missing opportunities for critical support in areas such as education, healthcare, financial assistance, and social security.

The Government Scheme Portal project addresses this problem by developing a unified, accessible platform that consolidates scheme information, implements intelligent search and conversational interfaces, supports multiple Indian languages, and provides personalized recommendations, thus democratizing access to welfare scheme information across demographic and technological divides.

% Methodology
\chapter{Methodology}
\thispagestyle{fancy}

\section{Detailed Plan of Action}
Our methodology combines user-centered design with agile development principles:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item User Research and Design:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Conduct user interviews with diverse demographic groups
        \item Create user personas representing different citizen profiles
        \item Develop journey maps and wireframes for testing
        \item Iterate designs based on usability feedback
    \end{itemize}
    
    \item Data Collection and Structuring:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Identify authoritative sources for scheme information
        \item Define standardized schema for scheme data
        \item Implement web scraping for government websites
        \item Develop data validation protocols
    \end{itemize}
    
    \item Technical Implementation:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Component-based architecture for maintainability
        \item Responsive design for cross-device compatibility
        \item RESTful APIs for data access
        \item Modular services for core functionality
    \end{itemize}
    
    \item Natural Language Processing:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Train models for understanding scheme queries
        \item Implement intent recognition for chatbot
        \item Develop entity extraction for user information
        \item Create dialogue management for conversations
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\section{Web Scraping Implementation}
\subsection{Overview of Web Scraping Process}
We implemented a comprehensive web scraping pipeline to aggregate government scheme information from multiple official sources. Our parser-based approach uses Python libraries to extract structured data from government websites while respecting ethical web scraping practices.

\subsection{Web Scraping Stack}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Python Libraries:} Requests for HTTP requests, BeautifulSoup for HTML parsing
    \item \textbf{Data Processing:} Pandas for data manipulation and storage
    \item \textbf{Ethical Considerations:} Implementation of delays and proper headers
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Parser-Based Scraping Workflow}
Our parser-based scraping workflow follows these steps:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Source Identification:} We identify and catalog relevant government websites containing scheme information
    \item \textbf{URL Collection:} We extract URLs for individual scheme pages from index/listing pages
    \item \textbf{HTML Fetching:} Using Python's requests library, we fetch the raw HTML content
    \item \textbf{DOM Parsing:} BeautifulSoup parses the HTML structure into navigable DOM objects
    \item \textbf{Data Extraction:} We extract specific elements like titles, descriptions, eligibility criteria, and benefits
    \item \textbf{Data Cleaning:} We clean and normalize the extracted text data
    \item \textbf{Structured Storage:} The cleaned data is stored in a structured format in our database
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{URL Extraction Process}
The first step in our data collection pipeline was extracting URLs for individual scheme pages:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python, caption=URL Extraction Code]
import requests
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup
import pandas as pd
import time

# Set headers to mimic browser behavior
headers = {
    'User-Agent': 'Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/91.0.4472.124 Safari/537.36',
    'Accept-Language': 'en-US,en;q=0.9',
    'Accept-Encoding': 'gzip, deflate, br',
    'Connection': 'keep-alive'
}

# List to store all scheme URLs
full_urls = []

# Main listing page URL
main_url = "https://example.gov.in/schemes"

# Get HTML content
response = requests.get(main_url, headers=headers)
soup = BeautifulSoup(response.content, 'html.parser')

# Extract all scheme links
scheme_links = soup.find_all('a', class_='scheme-link')
for link in scheme_links:
    url = link['href']
    full_urls.append(url)

# Save URLs to CSV
url_df = pd.DataFrame(full_urls, columns=['url'])
url_df.to_csv('scheme_urls.csv', index=False)
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Data Extraction from Individual Schemes}
For each extracted URL, we implemented a process to visit the page and extract specific scheme details:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python, caption=Scheme Data Extraction Code]
import pandas as pd
import requests
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup
import time

# Read URLs from previous step
urls_df = pd.read_csv('scheme_urls.csv')
scheme_urls = urls_df['url'].tolist()

# List to store all scheme data
all_schemes = []

# Process each URL
for url in scheme_urls:
    # Add delay to respect server resources
    time.sleep(1)
    
    response = requests.get(url, headers=headers)
    soup = BeautifulSoup(response.content, 'html.parser')
    
    # Extract scheme details using selectors
    try:
        scheme_title = soup.find('h1', class_='scheme-title').text.strip()
        scheme_desc = soup.find('div', class_='scheme-description').text.strip()
        scheme_eligibility = soup.find('div', class_='eligibility').text.strip()
        
        # Create data dictionary
        data = {
            'Title': scheme_title,
            'Description': scheme_desc,
            'Eligibility': scheme_eligibility,
            'URL': url
        }
        
        all_schemes.append(data)
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"Error processing {url}: {e}")

# Convert to DataFrame and save
schemes_df = pd.DataFrame(all_schemes)
schemes_df.to_csv('schemes_data.csv', index=False)
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Ethical Web Scraping Practices}
We implemented several measures to ensure ethical web scraping:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Request Delays:} We included a 1-second delay between requests using \texttt{time.sleep(1)} to avoid overwhelming servers and simulate human browsing behavior.
    \item \textbf{Browser Headers:} We used proper HTTP headers to identify our requests transparently.
    \item \textbf{Error Handling:} We implemented robust error handling to manage unexpected HTML structures or server responses.
    \item \textbf{Rate Limiting:} Our scraper was designed to respect the implicit rate limits of government websites.
\end{itemize}

\section{Chatbot Implementation}
\subsection{Chatbot Architecture}
We developed a conversational interface to help users discover relevant government schemes through natural language queries:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Frontend:} HTML, CSS, and JavaScript to create a responsive chat interface
    \item \textbf{Backend:} n8n webhook endpoint for processing queries
    \item \textbf{Language Support:} Hindi and English queries supported
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Chatbot Features}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Chat History:} Local storage for persisting conversation history across sessions
    \item \textbf{Typing Indicator:} Visual feedback showing when the bot is processing
    \item \textbf{Session Management:} Unique session IDs using UUID for tracking conversations
    \item \textbf{Error Handling:} Graceful responses when backend services are unavailable
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Conversation Flow}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item User enters a query about government schemes (e.g., "मुझे शिक्षा योजना चाहिए")
    \item Query is sent to the n8n webhook endpoint
    \item Backend processes the query using NLP techniques
    \item Relevant schemes are identified and formatted as a response
    \item Response is displayed to the user in the chat interface
\end{enumerate}

\section{Technology Stack}
\subsection{Frontend}
\begin{itemize}
    \item React.js for component-based UI development
    \item Tailwind CSS for responsive styling
    \item Accessibility components for inclusive design
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Backend}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Node.js with Express.js for API development
    \item Search service for query processing
    \item Chatbot service for conversational interface
    \item Language service for translations
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Database}
\begin{itemize}
    \item PostgreSQL via Supabase for structured data storage
    \item Analytics store for usage tracking
    \item Session management for user state
\end{itemize}

\subsection{External Services}
\begin{itemize}
    \item OpenAI API for NLP capabilities
    \item Bhashini API for language translation
    \item Web scraping services for data collection
\end{itemize}

\section{Project Timeline}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Phase 1: Requirements Gathering and Design (2 weeks)
    \item Phase 2: Database Development and Data Collection (3 weeks)
    \item Phase 3: Core Interface Implementation (4 weeks)
    \item Phase 4: Chatbot and Search Development (3 weeks)
    \item Phase 5: Language and Accessibility Features (2 weeks)
    \item Phase 6: Testing and Refinement (2 weeks)
\end{itemize}

